editorialMd:
Build the result list

root is the top node of a binary tree. Each node has a value and up to two children. The root is at depth 0, its children are at depth 1, and so on. Add the values at each depth. Put one sum per depth in the result list, starting at depth 0. Missing children add nothing. An empty tree gives an empty result list.

Return the answer

Read the result list from left to right. Return the first number that is at least k (greater than or equal to k). Return the number, not its position. If none matches, return -1.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the number of nodes. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function floorRegisterFirstQualifyingSlip(root, k) {
  const a=root; let out=[];
  if(a){const queue=[a];let head=0;while(head<queue.length){const end=queue.length;let s=0;while(head<end){const node=queue[head++];s+=node.val;if(node.left)queue.push(node.left);if(node.right)queue.push(node.right);}out.push(s);}}
  return out.find(x=>x>=k)??-1;
}


